---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Gaming" nav="life" description="Clash Royale at 12K+ trophies, chess at state level, and the one skill underneath: read the meta.">
  <header class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <a href="/#life" class="back">← Back to home</a>
      <div class="big" style="margin-top:22px">🎮</div>
      <h1>Gaming</h1>
      <p class="lede">Competitive by nature. Gaming has always been less about the game and more about the meta — understanding the system, finding the edge, executing under pressure.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="tight">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="eyebrow">Main game</div>
      <h2 style="margin-bottom:24px">Clash Royale</h2>
      <div class="bignums" style="max-width:520px;margin-bottom:36px">
        <div><b>12K+</b><span>Trophies</span></div>
        <div><b>3rd</b><span>College tournament</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="feat">
        <article><div class="n">The game</div><h3>Why Clash Royale specifically</h3><p>Real-time strategy with perfect information asymmetry — you don't know your opponent's deck until they play cards. Deck building, counter-meta reads, and split-second execution all matter. The ladder is a real competitive ecosystem.</p></article>
        <article><div class="n">The meta</div><h3>12,000+ trophies</h3><p>Reaching this trophy count puts you in the top percentile of global players. The ladder at this level is solved-ish — the variance is low, and consistent wins require genuine understanding of the meta, matchups, and tempo.</p></article>
        <article><div class="n">🏆 3rd place</div><h3>IITD College Clash Royale Tournament</h3><p>College-wide tournament bracket. Made it to the final three before losing out. Single-elimination at that stage — variance is high, execution pressure is real. One wrong card placement in a tight match and it's over.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="tight" style="background:var(--paper-2)">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="two">
        <div>
          <div class="eyebrow">Also plays</div>
          <h2 style="margin-bottom:14px">Chess</h2>
          <p class="lede">Competed at zonal and state level during school. Chess is where the pattern recognition habit started — reading positions, thinking in trees, playing the player as much as the board.</p>
          <div class="pills" style="margin-top:18px"><span>Zonal level competitor</span><span>State level competitor</span><span>Opening theory</span><span>Endgame technique</span><span>Blitz & rapid formats</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="call" style="margin-bottom:10px">Built · hollowchess.com</div>
          <h3>Hollow Chess</h3>
          <p>Standard chess, but the board itself is fair game. On board turns you lift an empty square and re-attach it somewhere else — tear a hole in a bishop's diagonal, grow a corridor behind the enemy king. The pieces play chess; the players play geography.</p>
          <a href="https://hollowchess.com" class="more" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Play it</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="tight">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="eyebrow">The thread</div>
      <h2 style="margin-bottom:12px">Gaming, trading, thinking</h2>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-bottom:32px">Every game worth playing has a meta. Understanding the meta — the optimal strategy given the current environment — and then either playing it or exploiting the people playing it, is a universally transferable skill. It shows up in markets, it shows up in poker, it shows up in how I approach new problems.</p>
      <div class="two">
        <div class="card"><div class="call" style="margin-bottom:10px">Skill</div><h3>Reading systems</h3><p>Every game has rules, incentives, and emergent dynamics. Finding out how those interact — faster than other players — is the actual game.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><div class="call" style="margin-bottom:10px">Skill</div><h3>Performing under pressure</h3><p>Late in a tournament, with something at stake, every decision gets heavier. Getting comfortable in that feeling is something you only learn by being in it.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
